'''''Abutilon grandifolium''''', the '''hairy Indian mallow''', is a large [[shrub]] that is up to {{convert|3|m|abbr=on|1}} high with broad, {{convert|3|-|18|cm|abbr=on|1}} leaf blades. Flowers are axillary, with a yellow corolla {{convert|2|-|3.5|cm|abbr=on|1}} across, composed of petals {{convert|1|-|1.5|cm|abbr=on|1}} long. The fruits are ovoid-globular [[schizocarp]]s that are {{convert|1|-|1.5|cm|abbr=on|1}} in diameter and composed of ten shortly beaked mericarps, containing 2-3 seeds each.<ref name=efloras>{{eFloras|5|242412002|Abutilon grandifolium|accessdate= 15 October 2015}}</ref>

''A. grandifolium'' can be distinguished from [[Abutilon theophrasti|''A. theophrasti'']] by long, simple hairs on the stem rather than [[Trichomes|stellate]] hairs.

==Distribution==
The species is native to tropical [[America]]<ref name=efloras/> and Central and South [[Africa]],<ref name=hortus>Bailey, L.H. and E.Z. Bailey, [https://www.worldcat.org/oclc/756410167 ''Hortus Third: A Concise Dictionary of Plants Cultivated in the United States and Canada''], MacMillan Publishing Co., Inc., New York (1977).</ref> but it is naturalised in other parts of the world, including the [[Canary Islands]], [[Hawaii]], and throughout [[shrubland]] and [[loam]]y areas of [[Australia]]. In Western Australia, it is found in the [[Swan Coastal Plain]].<ref name="florabase">{{cite web|title=''Abutilon grandifolium'' (Willd.) Sweet|url=http://florabase.calm.wa.gov.au/browse/profile/19708|url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080908053659/http://florabase.calm.wa.gov.au/browse/profile/19708|archive-date=September 8, 2008|access-date=March 10, 2008|publisher=[[FloraBase]]}}</ref> Due to this species’ prolific seed production and propensity to spread, ''A. grandifolium'' is considered an [[invasive species]] in some of the regions where it grows, becoming a problematic weed in [[riparian zone]]s, [[grassland]]s, and tall shrubland [[ecosystem]]s throughout the world. In Hawaii, it is reported as having a detrimental effect on ''[[Spermolepis hawaiiensis]]'' and ''[[Scaevola coriacea]],'' two endangered and threatened plant species.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cabi.org/isc/datasheet/111972#tosummaryOfInvasiveness|title=Abutilon grandifolium (hairy Indian mallow)|last=|first=|date=|website=CABI: Invasive Species Compendium - Detailed coverage of invasive species threatening livelihoods and the environment worldwide|archive-url=|archive-date=|access-date=10 April 2020}}</ref>

==Threat level==
''A. grandifolium'' is considered an invasive or potentially invasive weed in [[Micronesia]], [[Hawaii]], [[Portugal]], [[South Africa]], and in parts of [[Australia]] (particularly [[South East Queensland|southeastern Queensland]] and eastern [[New South Wales]]),<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cabi.org/isc/datasheet/111972|title=Abutilon grandifolium (hairy Indian mallow)|website=www.cabi.org|language=en|access-date=2020-04-15}}</ref> and is not considered a threatened species.<ref name=florabase/>

